diagnosis_text:
Router16 is up and operating normally. Its FRRouting (FRR) daemon is running, and OSPF adjacencies are established on both sides: to the campus L3 switch (mswitch0) over bond0 (10.2.2.1/24) and to the upstream core (router7) over eth2 (172.30.1.1/24). Evidence:
- router16: frr is active; “show ip ospf neighbor” shows Full adjacency with mswitch0 (neighbor ID 172.16.30.254 at 10.2.2.2 on bond0) and with router7 (neighbor ID 192.168.10.1 at 172.30.1.2 on eth2).
- router16 routing table contains 172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24, and 172.16.30.0/24 learned via OSPF from 10.2.2.2 (mswitch0).
- router7’s OSPF routes point to those same campus networks via 172.30.1.1 (router16), confirming upstream propagation.
- End-to-end reachability: pings from a datacenter host (dc_pc5, 10.5.0.5) to campus hosts 172.16.10.5 and 172.16.30.12 succeed with 0% loss; the topology-wide check also shows 172.16.20.9 reachable from multiple non-campus hosts.

Mechanism-wise, mswitch0 is the L3 gateway for VLANs 10/20/30 (SVIs 172.16.x.254) and advertises those via OSPF to router16, which in turn advertises them upstream—this entire chain is healthy now. Localization checks confirm healthy interfaces: router16 bond0 and eth2 are up.

Root cause: No current fault found; the earlier report likely reflected a transient or non-network issue not reproduced here.

Corrective fix: No changes needed. Recommend monitoring OSPF adjacencies on router16 (bond0, eth2) and mswitch0 for flaps; if users still report issues, collect timestamps to correlate with OSPF logs. Campus 1 is currently reachable. 
